WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.180
Which operations preserve context-free languages?

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:06.180
Union, concatenation and star have

3
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.060
grammar constructions, but arbitrary intersection

4
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.760
and complement do not. Intersection

5
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.120
with a regular language is

6
00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.080
a special positive case, and

7
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.460
we will show exactly why

8
00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.280
one stack still suffices there.

9
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.340
For union, rename component variables

10
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.900
and introduce a fresh start

11
00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:24.720
choosing either old start. For

12
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.820
concatenation, make the new start

13
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.620
produce the two component starts

14
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:31.200
in sequence. For star, use

15
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.740
a fresh start producing an

16
00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.700
old-start factor followed by itself,

17
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:36.960
or epsilon for zero factors.

18
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:39.520
Each construction has a two-way

19
00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:42.240
derivation argument. A generated word

20
00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.320
splits into the selected components

21
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.900
or factors, and conversely any

22
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.460
words from those components have

23
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.000
derivations that can be joined.

24
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.040
The raw combined grammar need

25
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.480
not be in Chomsky normal

26
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.300
form unless you convert it

27
00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:55.820
afterwards.

28
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:58.580
Consider the context-free language of

29
00:00:58.580 --> 00:00:59.880
equal a and b blocks

30
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.580
followed by any c block.

31
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:03.540
Also consider any a block

32
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.000
followed by equal b and

33
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.960
c blocks. Each has a

34
00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:08.980
simple grammar using one matched

35
00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.480
recursive pair and one unconstrained

36
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.260
repeated block.

37
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.400
A word in their intersection

38
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.220
must have all three ordered

39
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.720
block counts equal. The intersection

40
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.320
is therefore the three-equal-block language,

41
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.560
which the context-free pumping lemma

42
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.620
proved not context-free. This is

43
00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.900
a concrete counterexample to general

44
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.400
intersection closure, not merely a

45
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.700
failed attempt to find a

46
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.200
construction.

47
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.360
If context-free languages were also

48
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.640
closed under complement, union closure

49
00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.180
and De Morgan would make

50
00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.360
them closed under intersection. That

51
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.840
contradicts our counterexample. Therefore general

52
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.840
complement closure fails as well,

53
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.940
although this argument does not

54
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.700
claim the complement of every

55
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:54.440
individual CFL is non-context-free.

56
00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.120
For a context-free language C

57
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:59.540
and regular language R, combine

58
00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:01.700
a pushdown automaton for C

59
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.880
with a deterministic automaton for

60
00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:05.860
R. Store their control states

61
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:07.440
as one pair and retain

62
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:09.420
only the pushdown machine’s single

63
00:02:09.420 --> 00:02:11.320
stack. On a consumed symbol,

64
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.820
update both controls and perform

65
00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.260
the original stack action.

66
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.280
On an epsilon input move

67
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.440
of the pushdown machine, keep

68
00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.360
the deterministic state fixed. No

69
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:25.160
input symbol has been consumed

70
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.500
for the regular filter to

71
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.020
read. The product’s invariant records

72
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.580
the PDA run and the

73
00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:32.020
DFA run on the same

74
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.800
consumed prefix, proving it recognizes

75
00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.820
their intersection.

76
00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.900
Trying the same construction with

77
00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.260
two pushdown automata would require

78
00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.860
two independent stacks. A single

79
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.520
PDA does not provide that

80
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.340
memory model, and the intersection

81
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.500
counterexample shows that no general

82
00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.440
one-stack repair can exist. The

83
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.500
regular component is special precisely

84
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.440
because its memory fits inside

85
00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:55.260
finite control.

86
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:57.980
Apply the regular-filter result to

87
00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.720
words with equal numbers of

88
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.280
a, b and c in

89
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.400
any order. Intersect with a

90
00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:04.760
star b star c star

91
00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:06.060
to force the ordered form,

92
00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:08.900
obtaining the known non-context-free equal-block

93
00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:11.120
language. If the original language

94
00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.480
were context-free the filtered result

95
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:14.960
would be too, giving a

96
00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:15.420
contradiction.

97
00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.460
Things to take note of.

98
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.300
Prove positive closure by constructions

99
00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.960
and negative closure by actual

100
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.180
counterexample languages. Preserve epsilon semantics

101
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.980
in the PDA-DFA product, distinguish

102
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:29.760
one stack from two, and

103
00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.440
do not confuse nonclosure of

104
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.560
a class with a claim

105
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.680
about every particular language pair.
